From 3588e9ca985c3a42b49bc04b6bfd3259a23e06d6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Fri, 4 Sep 2026 14:19:49 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 11074/11171] Record that the GPU allocation has no
 enforcement, and I broke it too

CLAUDE.md records a 2026-06-27 allocation giving the conductor GPU 0 and the
outer loop GPU 1 to stop contention. Both sides routinely take both cards.

The conductor's environment sets CARNOT_ARC_GENERATOR_CUDA_GPU=0 and does not set
CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES at all, verified from /proc. That variable governs the ARC
generator, not a torch task, so an experiment's own
os.environ.setdefault("CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES", "0,1") wins by default. The pattern
appears across 141 files.

Caught in both directions. A conductor task is spanning both cards right now at
11,374 and 10,908 MiB. My own r11l eval yesterday did the same -- one llama-server
pid on both GPUs at 10,542 and 11,364 -- and I reported it as running on GPU 1 for
seven hours. It was not.

No collision has been observed and none is claimed. The finding is that nothing
prevents one: a dual-card conductor task launched against a seven-hour eval
holding both cards means one of them fails or crawls, and the allocation written
to prevent that has no enforcement point.

It stayed invisible because the dashboard prints per-GPU totals rather than
per-process ownership, so two processes each taking both cards looks exactly like
two processes each taking one. I saw it only because GPU 1 read 100% at a moment
I knew I had nothing running.

Not fixed. Exporting CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES=0 for conductor children is one line and
would break any experiment that genuinely needs 48GB; fixing 141 call sites is
correct and large; teaching the dashboard per-process ownership makes contention
visible without deciding anything. Which one depends on whether any current
experiment needs both cards, which is the operator's to know.

