+
+## 2026-09-04 — the conductor/outer-loop GPU split is a convention with nothing enforcing it
+
+CLAUDE.md records a 2026-06-27 operator allocation: the conductor owns GPU 0, the outer loop owns
+GPU 1, "to stop GPU contention". Observed today: both sides routinely take both cards.
+
+**The mechanism.** The conductor's own environment sets `CARNOT_ARC_GENERATOR_CUDA_GPU=0` and
+**does not set `CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES` at all** — verified from `/proc/1301127/environ`. That env var
+governs the ARC generator only, not a torch task. Individual experiment scripts then do
+
+```python
+os.environ.setdefault("CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES", "0,1")
+```
+
+and because the parent set nothing, the `setdefault` wins. `CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES` appears in 141
+files under `scripts/` and `python/carnot/`.
+
+**Caught in the act, both directions.** Right now `carnot.experiment_6986_three_family_contrast_
+features` — a conductor task — is spanning both cards at 11,374 MiB and 10,908 MiB with
+`CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES=0,1`. And my own r11l eval yesterday did the same: its llama-server, one pid,
+appeared on both GPUs at 10,542 and 11,364 MiB. I reported that run as "on GPU 1" for seven hours.
+It was not.
+
+**No collision has been observed**, and I am not claiming one. The two sides have not so far wanted
+both cards at the same moment. The finding is that nothing prevents it: if the conductor launches a
+dual-card torch task while a seven-hour eval holds both, one of them fails or crawls, and the
+allocation that exists to prevent exactly that has no enforcement point.
+
+**Why it stayed invisible.** The dashboard prints per-GPU totals, not per-process ownership, so two
+processes each quietly taking both cards looks identical to two processes each taking one. I only
+saw it because GPU 1 showed 100% utilisation at a moment when I knew I had no job running.
+
+**Not fixed — the options differ in blast radius.** The conductor could export
+`CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES=0` for its children, which is one line and would immediately break any
+experiment that genuinely needs both cards for a large model. Or the 141 call sites could stop
+defaulting to `0,1`, which is correct and large. Or the allocation could be acknowledged as advisory
+and the dashboard taught to show per-process GPU ownership so contention is at least visible. That
+is an operator's call and depends on whether any current experiment actually needs 48 GB.
